Andrew Fabbro wrote:
> 
> When a chameleon imitates a monster, does it gain all of that
> monster's powers?  (i.e., is it a true polymorph?)

Yes, it gains the properties of the imitated monsters.

> Seems like it could be a useful pet.  Sure, part of the time you'd
> have a newt, but part of the time you'd have an arch-lich...

In my games chameleons typically die very fast; I prefer other pets.

> Also seems to me like it could be a very nasty opponent.  You walk up
> on a newt and whack it, then find you are looking at a master mind
> flayer. 

True, but see it also the other way round; if you see an out-of-depth
monster[*] you just need to survive a couple turns until it becomes
something wimpy. Engraving Elbereth fixes the problem in most cases.

> Is there a way to detect whether a creature is a chameleon?
> Does a stethoscope reveal this?

A ring of protection from shape changers will reveal it's real form.
(Never tried the stethoscope on those monsters.)

Janis

[*] And if it has not changed shape through a polytrap; that's hard
to distinguish, but if there are more out-of-depth monsters on the
level it has more likely been a trap. Or just observe the monster a
couple turns from a safe spot.


